## Find the probability of each choice
One letter out of 26 is right, and each number is one out of 10.
P(M, 3, 3) &= \frac{1}{26}\times\frac{1}{10}\times\frac{1}{10}
&= \frac{1}{2600}
ANSWER: \frac{1}{2600}

## Probabilities of the events
There are $26 \times 10 \times 10 = 2600$ outcomes.
Jackpot: $\frac{1}{2600}$. Letter and first number only: $1 \times 1 \times 9 = 9$ outcomes, so $\frac{9}{2600}$. Letter and second number only: also $\frac{9}{2600}$. Letter only: $1 \times 9 \times 9 = 81$ outcomes, so $\frac{81}{2600}$.
## Multiply payout by probability
\text{Jackpot: } x\,P(x) &= 1000 \times \frac{1}{2600} = \frac{1000}{2600}
\text{Letter, first number: } x\,P(x) &= 50 \times \frac{9}{2600} = \frac{450}{2600}
\text{Letter, second number: } x\,P(x) &= 50 \times \frac{9}{2600} = \frac{450}{2600}
\text{Letter only: } x\,P(x) &= 50 \times \frac{81}{2600} = \frac{4050}{2600}
\text{Fail to win: } x\,P(x) &= 0
## Add to find the expected payout
\sum x\,P(x) &= \frac{1000}{2600}+\frac{450}{2600}+\frac{450}{2600}+\frac{4050}{2600}
&= \frac{5950}{2600}
&\approx 2.29
## Compare with the €2 charge
The club pays out $\text{€}2.29$ on average per play but takes in $\text{€}2$.
2 - 2.29 &= -0.29
ANSWER: \text{Club loses } 29 \text{ cent per play}

## Find the diagonal CE of the rectangular base
$CDEF$ is a rectangle with $|CD| = 2.5$ and $|CF| = |DE| = 3$, so $CE$ is the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle.
|CE|^2 &= 3^2 + 2.5^2
&= 9 + 6.25
&= 15.25
|CE| &= \sqrt{15.25}
&\approx 3.905
## Halve the diagonal to get AC
$A$ is where the diagonals cross, so it is the midpoint of $CE$.
|AC| &= \tfrac{1}{2}\sqrt{15.25}
&\approx 1.9525
&\approx 1.95\text{ m}
ANSWER: |AC| = 1.95\text{ m}

## Use tan in the right-angled triangle BAC
The angle of elevation is $\angle BCA = 50^\circ$. $|AB|$ is opposite it and $|AC| = 1.95$ is adjacent.
\tan 50^\circ &= \frac{|AB|}{1.95}
## Solve for AB
|AB| &= 1.95 \tan 50^\circ
&= 1.95(1.19175)
&= 2.3239
&\approx 2.3\text{ m}
ANSWER: |AB| = 2.3\text{ m}

## Use Pythagoras in the right-angled triangle BAC
$|AB| = 2.3$ and $|AC| = 1.95$ are the two shorter sides; $|BC|$ is the hypotenuse.
|BC|^2 &= 1.95^2 + 2.3^2
&= 3.8025 + 5.29
&= 9.0925
## Take the square root
|BC| &= \sqrt{9.0925}
&\approx 3.0154
&\approx 3\text{ m}
ANSWER: |BC| = 3\text{ m}

## Find AC using tan 60°
$|AB| = 3$ is opposite the $60^\circ$ angle and $|CA|$ is adjacent.
\tan 60^\circ &= \frac{3}{|CA|}
|CA| &= \frac{3}{\tan 60^\circ}
&= \frac{3}{\sqrt{3}}
&= \sqrt{3}
## Double it to get the diagonal CE
|CE| &= 2|CA|
&= 2\sqrt{3}
## Use Pythagoras with the square of side x
The two sides of the square and the diagonal $CE$ form a right-angled triangle.
x^2 + x^2 &= (2\sqrt{3})^2
2x^2 &= 12
x^2 &= 6
x &= \sqrt{6}
ANSWER: \sqrt{6}\text{ m}

## Find the period
For $\cos(bt)$ the period is $\dfrac{2\pi}{b}$. Here $b = \dfrac{\pi}{6}$.
\text{Period} &= \frac{2\pi}{\pi/6}
&= 2\pi \times \frac{6}{\pi}
&= 12\text{ hours}
## Find the range
$\cos$ always lies between $-1$ and $1$, so the $1.5\cos(\cdot)$ term lies between $-1.5$ and $1.5$.
\text{Minimum} &= 1.6 - 1.5
&= 0.1
\text{Maximum} &= 1.6 + 1.5
&= 3.1
ANSWER: \text{Period} = 12\text{ hours},\quad \text{Range} = [0.1,\,3.1]\text{ m}

## Use the largest value of cos
The height is greatest when $\cos\left(\dfrac{\pi}{6}t\right) = 1$, which happens at $t = 0$.
h_{\max} &= 1.6 + 1.5(1)
&= 3.1\text{ m}
ANSWER: 3.1\text{ m}

## Differentiate h(t)
The rate of change of height is $h'(t)$. The constant $1.6$ differentiates to $0$. Use the chain rule on $\cos\left(\dfrac{\pi}{6}t\right)$.
h'(t) &= 1.5\left(-\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}t\right)\right)\cdot\frac{\pi}{6}
&= -\frac{\pi}{4}\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}t\right)
## Substitute t = 2
h'(2) &= -\frac{\pi}{4}\sin\left(\frac{2\pi}{6}\right)
&= -\frac{\pi}{4}\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{3}\right)
&= -\frac{\pi}{4}\times\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}
&\approx -0.68\text{ m/h}
## Explain the answer
The rate is negative, so the height is falling. At $t = 2$ (2 a.m.) the tide is going out at $0.68$ m per hour.
ANSWER: h'(2) \approx -0.68\text{ m/h}

## Read the low and high tide from the graph
High tide is the highest point, $3.1$ m. Low tide is the lowest point, $0.1$ m.
\text{Difference} &= 3.1 - 0.1
&= 3\text{ m}
ANSWER: 3\text{ m}

## Find z₂ − z₃
z_2 - z_3 &= (2 - i) - (5 + i)
&= -3 - 2i
## Find its modulus
|z_2 - z_3| &= \sqrt{(-3)^2 + (-2)^2}
&= \sqrt{9 + 4}
&= \sqrt{13}
## Find z₁ + z₂
z_1 + z_2 &= (1 + 3i) + (2 - i)
&= 3 + 2i
## Find its modulus
|z_1 + z_2| &= \sqrt{3^2 + 2^2}
&= \sqrt{9 + 4}
&= \sqrt{13}
## Compare
Both moduli equal $\sqrt{13}$, so the statement is true.
ANSWER: |z_2 - z_3| = \sqrt{13} = |z_1 + z_2|
